{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\npaper=json.loads(Path('paper_data.json').read_text()); print('Tasks:',paper.get('task_names')); print('Downstream:',paper.get('downstream_study')); final=paper['final_evaluation']; print('Final keys:', list(final)); print('Qualification:',final.get('qualification')); print('Metrics:',final.get('metrics')); print('Strategy figures:',json.dumps(json.loads(Path('paper_strategy.json').read_text())['figure_specs'],indent=2)); print('Source files:',[str(path) for path in Path('source_evidence').rglob('*') if path.is_file()][:30])\nPY\nsed -n '1,95p' build_paper_artifacts.py\ncat draft_reference_gaps.md\ncat postdraft_reference_query_plan.md\nls .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts\ncat paper_evidence_audit_feedback.md\ncat scientific-evidence/verification-report.json | head -30\"","aggregated_output":"Tasks: None\nDownstream: None\nFinal keys: ['correct', 'evaluation_evidence', 'evaluator_sha256', 'limitations', 'manifest_path', 'metrics', 'outcome', 'program_tree_sha256', 'results_tree_sha256', 'status', 'task_tree_sha256']\nQualification: None\nMetrics: {'anchor_mae': 0.22047382735923254, 'combined_score': 0.9119853414564072, 'constant_mae': 0.22204546910278564, 'control_failure_rate': 0.041666666666666664, 'copy_mae': 0.15448451324975213, 'correct': 0, 'delta_anchor_ci_high': 1.0, 'delta_anchor_ci_low': -1.0, 'delta_anchor_mae': -0.0769036132600842, 'irt_mae': 0.1186668212839868, 'kendall_tau_b': 0.5943160224743321, 'mae': 0.08801465854359276, 'missing_episode_rate': 0.011049723756906077, 'no_calibration_mae': 0.10836174574059959, 'no_diversity_mae': 0.044910877034389785, 'permuted_mae': 0.1005045540030643, 'reset_mae': 0.02807015578570595, 'reset_max_abs_error': 0.0, 'rmse': 0.09789336720441476, 'source_mean_mae': 0.1643825693696333, 'spearman': 0.7328732236748521, 'uniform_mae': 0.1335974367939037}\nStrategy figures: [\n  {\n    \"figure_id\": \"method_and_evidence_overview\",\n    \"required\": true,\n    \"purpose\": \"Make the method and its evidential boundary understandable before the results; show that selection and tuning use source records only and final missing outputs are not prediction errors.\",\n    \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n    \"group_by\": \"information_flow\",\n    \"metric\": \"none; conceptual mechanism and evidence-status separation\",\n    \"split_by\": \"source fitting versus target observation versus evaluation\",\n    \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n    \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n    \"layout_rationale\": \"paper.sty specifies one 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height; full width and column width coincide. Place a regular figure at 1.0 linewidth on page 1 or 2, approximately 5.5 by 1.9 inches plus caption. Use at most six short blocks in two rows, no more than three blocks per row, and at least 9-point final-size text. Visually separate the ridge and optional particle branches without suggesting a newly evaluated architecture. Do not add quantitative arrows implying improvement.\",\n    \"allow_progression\": false,\n    \"max_panels\": 1,\n    \"max_panels_per_row\": 1,\n    \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n    \"max_series_per_panel\": 3\n  },\n  {\n    \"figure_id\": \"available_pair_method_comparison\",\n    \"required\": true,\n    \"purpose\": \"Compare measured prediction error among primary, fitted uniform selection, anchor, and IRT while keeping incomplete records visible as unavailable evidence.\",\n    \"figure_type\": \"grouped_dot_plot\",\n    \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n    \"group_by\": \"benchmark\",\n    \"metric\": \"MAE averaged equally over the three budgets on common available case-seed support; lower is better\",\n    \"split_by\": \"Open LLM Leaderboard-derived versus HELM-derived fixed cases\",\n    \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n    \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n    \"layout_rationale\": \"Use the inspected 5.5-inch single-column text width, approximately 3.8 inches high plus caption, with two vertically stacked horizontal dot plots. The panels contain four and six benchmark categories, respectively; share a clearly labeled linear MAE scale and a single four-method legend. Show seed points and an explicitly descriptive available-seed mean, not confidence bars. Annotate common-support seed counts and missing-record marks outside the numerical axis; never plot a penalty of one as a measured point. Keep final text at least 8.5 points and reserve caption space for conditional-support warnings. If dynamic range obscures the primary comparison, simplify or move absolute-value detail to the accompanying table rather than shrink labels or conceal the anchor comparator.\",\n    \"allow_progression\": false,\n    \"max_panels\": 2,\n    \"max_panels_per_row\": 1,\n    \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n    \"max_series_per_panel\": 4\n  },\n  {\n    \"figure_id\": \"budget_accuracy_and_ranking\",\n    \"required\": true,\n    \"purpose\": \"Separate the role of item selection from fitted reconstruction across the observed query budgets, with both score error and ranking quality.\",\n    \"figure_type\": \"grouped_dot_plot\",\n    \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n    \"group_by\": \"query_budget\",\n    \"metric\": \"MAE and Spearman correlation in separate panels, with direction labeled separately\",\n    \"split_by\": \"score error versus ranking quality\",\n    \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n    \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n    \"layout_rationale\": \"At the actual 5.5-inch text width, use two side-by-side panels approximately 2.6 inches wide within a 5.5 by 2.5-inch canvas, plus one shared four-series legend and caption. Each panel has only three budget categories and four methods: primary, fitted uniform selection, selected-item observed mean, and uniform sample mean. Use at least 8.5-point final-size labels and no connecting development trajectories. Compute both panels on the same available case-seed intersection for all displayed methods, retain the documented equal-source-family/case/seed/checkpoint hierarchy as applicable, and explicitly describe within-case support normalization. Show seed-level summaries; the appendix retains per-case values. Caption this as conditional descriptive reaggregation, not the official final aggregate or an independent confirmation.\",\n    \"allow_progression\": false,\n    \"max_panels\": 2,\n    \"max_panels_per_row\": 2,\n    \"max_categories_per_panel\": 3,\n    \"max_series_per_panel\": 4\n  }\n]\nSource files: ['source_evidence/selected_method.py', 'source_evidence/METHOD.md', 'source_evidence/snapshot_manifest.json', 'source_evidence/README.md', 'source_evidence/final_data_manifest.json', 'source_evidence/evaluate_final.py', 'source_evidence/program_spec.toml']\nimport json\nimport csv\nimport re\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use('Agg')\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\nfrom PIL import Image, ImageDraw, ImageFont\nfrom scipy.stats import spearmanr, kendalltau\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nFIGURES = ROOT / 'figures'\nTABLES = ROOT / 'tables'\nCASES = ['bbh', 'gpqa', 'musr', 'openllm_math', 'commonsense', 'gsm', 'legalbench', 'math', 'med_qa', 'mmlu']\nLABELS = ['BBH', 'GPQA', 'MuSR', 'OL-Math', 'Commonsense', 'GSM', 'LegalBench', 'MATH', 'MedQA', 'MMLU']\nCOLORS = ['#2166ac', '#d95f02', '#7570b3', '#419a75']\n\n\ndef provenance(path, inputs=None):\n    record = dict(schema_name='scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest',\n                  artifact=dict(path=str(path.relative_to(ROOT)), kind='figure' if path.suffix in ['.pdf', '.png'] else 'derived'),\n                  derived_from=inputs or ['@evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]'],\n                  generated_by=dict(script_path='build_paper_artifacts.py', command=['python', 'build_paper_artifacts.py']))\n    Path(str(path) + '.prov.json').write_text(json.dumps(record, indent=2))\n\n\ndef export(figure, stem):\n    for extension in ['pdf', 'png']:\n        path = FIGURES / f'{stem}.{extension}'\n        figure.savefig(path, dpi=300)\n        provenance(path)\n    plt.close(figure)\n\n\ndef predictions(case, method):\n    if method in case['conditions']:\n        condition = case['conditions'][method]\n        if not condition['completed']:\n            return None\n        values = condition['predictions']\n    else:\n        values = (case['baseline_predictions'] or {}).get(method)\n    return None if values is None else np.asarray(values, dtype=float)\n\n\ndef main():\n    FIGURES.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    TABLES.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    # @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    data = json.loads((ROOT / 'paper_data.json').read_text())\n    episodes = data['final_evaluation']['evaluation_evidence']['episodes']\n    seeds = [episode['seed'] for episode in episodes]\n    methods = ['primary', 'no_diversity', 'anchor', 'irt']\n    budget_methods = ['primary', 'no_diversity', 'no_calibration', 'uniform']\n    method_values = np.full((len(CASES), len(seeds), len(methods)), np.nan)\n    errors = np.full((len(CASES), len(seeds), len(budget_methods), 3), np.nan)\n    ranks = errors.copy()\n    support = {}\n    audit = []\n    complete_rows = []\n    primary_rows = []\n    for case_index, name in enumerate(CASES):\n        support[name] = []\n        for seed_index, episode in enumerate(episodes):\n            case = episode['solution']['cases'][name]\n            truth = np.asarray(case['target_full_means'])\n            for method in list(case['conditions']) + ['anchor', 'irt', 'uniform', 'source_mean']:\n                values = predictions(case, method)\n                condition = case['conditions'].get(method)\n                for budget_index, budget in enumerate(episode['solution']['budgets']):\n                    row = dict(case=name, seed=episode['seed'], budget=budget, method=method,\n                               target_count=len(truth), target_group_count=len(set(case['target_lineages'])),\n                               available=values is not None,\n                               structural_failure=condition['structural_failure'] if condition else None,\n                               mae=None, rmse=None, spearman=None, kendall_tau_b=None)\n                    if values is not None:\n                        predicted = values[budget_index]\n                        row['mae'] = float(np.abs(predicted - truth).mean())\n                        row['rmse'] = float(np.sqrt(np.mean((predicted - truth) ** 2)))\n                        row['spearman'] = float(spearmanr(truth, predicted).statistic) if np.ptp(predicted) > 0 and np.ptp(truth) > 0 else 0.0\n                        row['kendall_tau_b'] = float(kendalltau(truth, predicted).statistic) if np.ptp(predicted) > 0 and np.ptp(truth) > 0 else 0.0\n                    complete_rows.append(row)\n            primary = case['conditions']['primary']\n            metrics_text = ' & '.join(f'{primary[metric]:.4f}' for metric in ['mae', 'rmse', 'spearman', 'kendall_tau_b']) if primary['completed'] else r'\\multicolumn{4}{c}{Missing; not a measured loss}'\n            primary_rows.append(f'{LABELS[case_index]} & {episode[\"seed\"]} & {len(truth)} & {len(set(case[\"target_lineages\"]))} & {metrics_text}' + r' \\\\')\n            arrays = [predictions(case, method) for method in methods]\n            if all(array is not None for array in arrays):\n                support[name].append(episode['seed'])\n                for method_index, array in enumerate(arrays):\n                    method_values[case_index, seed_index, method_index] = np.abs(array - truth).mean()\n            arrays = [predictions(case, method) for method in budget_methods]\n            if all(array is not None for array in arrays):\n                for method_index, array in enumerate(arrays):\n# Draft Reference Gaps\n\n**Status:** ready\n\nTwo paragraph-specific gaps justify a small retrieval pass for Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark context and direct Rasch/IRT foundations. Existing related-work coverage needs no expansion. Both targets are medium priority; neither requires changing the paper's evidence-bounded thesis.\n\n## Grounded Review\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, all 25 entries in `references.bib` and their existing provenance, the 30 claims in `claims_grounding.json`, the structure and final-evaluation evidence in `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. All manuscript citation keys resolve, and every existing bibliography entry is cited. Global memory was used only as qualitative context, not numerical evidence. No references were retrieved.\n\n## 1. Benchmark Collection Context\n\n- **Target ID:** `evaluation-leaderboard-context`\n- **Section:** Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability\n- **Paragraph:** Fixed Benchmarks and Grouped Model Holdouts, opening paragraph.\n- **Priority / intent:** medium / benchmark_context\n- **Anchor:** “four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math)”\n- **Gap:** The paragraph identifies a named upstream collection without a direct collection reference; no dedicated Leaderboard source appears in the bibliography. HELM is already represented by `Liang2023`, which can be reused here without new retrieval. `Wang2024` concerns MMLU-Pro and must not be repurposed as provenance for the staged MMLU case.\n- **Focused search topic:** Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark collection documentation covering BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math.\n- **Bounded intervention:** Add one authoritative collection citation. Retain the staged names and the explicit warning against assuming public-benchmark identity. Case counts, fixed item universes, snapshot identity, and filtering remain supported by the supplied artifacts, not by this citation. Do not commission separate searches for all ten benchmarks.\n\n## 2. Named Item-Response Model Foundations\n\n- **Target ID:** `method-rasch-irt-foundations`\n- **Section:** Method\n- **Paragraph:** Linear and Residual-Profile Reconstruction, introduction of Rasch smoothing.\n- **Priority / intent:** medium / background\n- **Anchor:** “The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model.”\n- **Gap:** The named response family lacks a direct foundational citation. `Andersen1970` is cited in Related Work for an estimation procedure explicitly not used here, and its stored abstract addresses conditional estimation. Broad statistical texts and the already cited LLM compression methods do not directly anchor the Rasch/2PL distinction in these paragraphs. The local IRT comparator in Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability also introduces two-parameter logistic responses and fitted item information without a direct foundation citation.\n- **Focused search topics:** Rasch logistic ability–difficulty model foundations; two-parameter logistic discrimination and Fisher item information.\n- **Bounded intervention:** Retrieve one authoritative source covering both families, or at most two complementary sources. Cite the Rasch introduction and reuse the relevant source at the local IRT comparator if supported. Preserve the fixed-ability, penalized custom-fit description and the disclaimer that local references are not official reproductions. No claim of profile-component benefit or target-calibrated uncertainty should be added.\n\n## Coverage That Does Not Justify Retrieval\n\n- Related Work directly cites Anchor Points, tinyBenchmarks, metabench, reference-instance prediction, and active evaluation acquisition. There is no missing comparator-family paragraph to expand.\n- Scalar-target design, ridge regression, validation dependence, domain shift, missingness, ranking statistics, and bootstrap background already have relevant citations. The selector is explicitly a heuristic, not an unsupported optimality theorem.\n- Numerical accuracy, availability masks, failed qualification, structural diagnostics, and local implementation details are grounded in the supplied evidence. More citations cannot repair missing predictions, isolate a profile contribution, or establish overall superiority.\n- Do not add literature on deployment speedups, new benchmark populations, target-adaptive policies, or calibrated stopping rules: those would expand rather than support the existing story.\n\n## Handoff\n\nRetrieve approximately two to three sources total across these two targets, preferring reuse when one IRT source covers both model families. This memo does not prescribe new paper sections or a broad rewrite. The manuscript, bibliography, and reference provenance remain unchanged.\n# Post-Draft Reference Query Plan\n\n**Status:** ready  \n**Scope:** Three focused query specifications for the two medium-priority gaps in `draft_reference_gaps.md` and `draft_reference_gaps.json`. Query 3 is conditional. A later retrieval pass should add approximately two to three unique sources, not refresh the bibliography. No references were retrieved in this stage.\n\n## Central Topic\n\nEvidence-bounded prediction of LLM full binary benchmark scores and rankings from fixed 8-, 16-, or 32-item prefixes using source-only score-covariance selection and ridge/Rasch-residual reconstruction. Only upstream Open LLM Leaderboard collection context and direct Rasch/2PL foundations need incremental citation support.\n\n## Grounding and Existing Coverage\n\nThe gap memo and JSON were read first and drive every query below. The manuscript, all 25 existing bibliography entries and their stored provenance, the original query-plan schema, and the global memory summary were checked locally. Global memory supplies qualitative context only, not numerical evidence.\n\n- **Core methods:** Fixed source-only prefixes, grouped tuning, and ridge prediction already have adequate methodological context. The Rasch introduction at `paper.tex:86` needs direct model-family support; its fixed-ability, group-weighted penalized fit and optional profile blend remain custom, artifact-grounded details.\n- **Comparators:** The local 2PL comparator at `paper.tex:130` and its information formula at `paper.tex:293` need foundational support. Anchor Points, tinyBenchmarks, metabench, reference-instance prediction, and active evaluation acquisition already have direct citations; do not expand their coverage.\n- **Benchmarks:** One collection reference is missing for the four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases named at `paper.tex:115`. For the six HELM-derived cases in that paragraph, reuse `Liang2023` without retrieval. `Wang2024` addresses MMLU-Pro and is not provenance for staged MMLU.\n- **Architectural shifts:** None are requested or justified.\n- **Result-driven boundaries:** Missing predictions, failed qualification, structural diagnostics, and retained-support results remain artifact-grounded. Citations cannot repair those outcomes, isolate a profile contribution, establish selector superiority, or calibrate target uncertainty.\n\nThe original plan already included a Leaderboard collection query, but no dedicated collection source is present in the resulting bibliography or provenance. Reopening that specific unresolved gap is warranted; rerunning successful comparator searches is not. The stored abstract for `Andersen1970` concerns conditional maximum-likelihood estimation, explicitly distinguished from this implementation, rather than directly supporting both response-model families.\n\n## Queries\n\n### 1. Upstream Leaderboard Collection Context\n\n- **Query:** `Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark collection documentation BBH GPQA MuSR OpenLLM Math`\n- **Category:** `benchmark_context`\n- **Gap / location:** `evaluation-leaderboard-context`; Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability / Fixed Benchmarks and Grouped Model Holdouts, opening paragraph, `paper.tex:115`.\n- **Anchor:** “four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math)”\n- **Rationale:** Seek one authoritative collection source documenting the upstream benchmark composition, closing the unresolved collection citation gap. Do not use it to substantiate staged counts, filtering, exact snapshot identity, or equivalence with public benchmark versions. Reuse existing HELM support instead of searching again; do not substitute MMLU-Pro provenance for staged MMLU.\n- **Completion condition:** One verified collection source supports the named upstream context. Preserve staged case names and the manuscript's explicit non-equivalence warning; do not commission separate searches for every benchmark.\n\n### 2. Rasch Response-Model Foundations\n\n- **Query:** `Rasch logistic item-response model ability item difficulty foundational reference`\n- **Category:** `core_method`\n- **Gap / location:** `method-rasch-irt-foundations`; Method / Linear and Residual-Profile Reconstruction, `paper.tex:86`.\n- **Anchor:** “The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model.”\n- **Rationale:** Seek an authoritative foundation for logistic ability-minus-difficulty responses. Prefer a source also covering 2PL discrimination and item information so it can support the later comparator paragraph. Existing `Andersen1970` supports a distinction about conditional estimation, not this full response-family framing. Broad statistical texts and already-cited LLM compression methods do not directly close the identified gap.\n- **Completion condition:** One verified source supports the Rasch model family. Keep the fixed-ability penalized fitting description explicit; do not portray it as standard joint estimation or cite foundations as evidence of a measured profile benefit.\n\n### 3. Conditional 2PL and Item-Information Complement\n\n- **Query:** `two-parameter logistic item-response model discrimination Fisher item information authoritative reference`\n- **Category:** `baseline_comparator`\n- **Gap / location:** Same `method-rasch-irt-foundations` target; Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability / Comparators and Control Semantics, `paper.tex:130`, and Supplementary Information / Local Reference Implementations, `paper.tex:293`.\n- **Anchor:** “The IRT reference fits a penalized two-parameter logistic response model, chooses items by fitted information near zero ability, and infers target ability from queried responses.”\n- **Rationale:** Seek at most one complementary authoritative source for variable discrimination and Fisher item information `a^2 p(1-p)`, verifying consistency with the draft's `theta*a-d` parameterization. This is foundational support for the already-written local comparator, not a new comparator-family search or certification of an official reproduction.\n- **Execution / completion condition:** Run only if the source from Query 2 does not cover the required 2PL distinction and information expression. Otherwise reuse that source and skip this query. Retain all local-fit, optimizer, and non-reproduction disclaimers.\n\n## Exclusions and Stop Rule\n\n- Do not broaden into a bibliography rebalance, generic benchmark-compression search, citation-graph traversal, related-paper recommendations, or searches for all ten benchmarks. Do not repeat HELM or successful comparator retrieval directions.\n- Retain the original plan's prohibition on identifying or accessing the task-prohibited unnamed paper or its authors, title, findings, mirrors, summaries, reviews, presentations, repositories, or discussions. No such identification is needed for these gaps.\n- Do not equate an upstream collection with staged item universes, filtering, counts, snapshot identity, or public benchmark versions; do not conflate staged MMLU with MMLU-Pro.\n- Do not add latent